# Exhaustiveness: two apps share apps/kustomize-app on purpose (see the catalog), so
# both are selected by the comment-only edit - and NOTHING changes in either render,
# which is the point: this case shows the label asymmetry with no diff to explain it.
# No resources= line, because no resource moved.
expect:affected=2 changed=0
# THE MIRROR OF case/lint-policy-added. There the base side had no policies; here the
# branch DELETES them, so the TARGET side is the unlinted one. The pair must appear
# with the sides swapped:
#
# a finding on BASE, where the policies still exist...
must:\[base\] \[lint-kyverno#1/images-must-be-pinned\] Deployment/kust-web
# ...and the note on TARGET, where they no longer do.
must:\[target\] \[lint-kyverno#1\] not linted: no policies in "policies/kyverno-removed"
# This is what the direction buys. The violation PRE-EXISTS on both sides - kustomize-app
# carries nginx:latest on master - and nothing about it changed, so a reader who trusts
# the label vocabulary alone would read a [base]-only finding as "fixed by this change".
# It was not fixed; it stopped being checked. The note is the only thing that says so,
# and lint-policy-added cannot demonstrate this half: its violation is introduced by
# its own branch.
must-not:^\+.*image:
must-not:^-.*image:
# The note must be one-sided, or it marks no asymmetry: base really was linted, which
# the finding above proves, so the note must not appear there too.
must-not:\[base\] \[lint-kyverno#1\] not linted
# And the target side cannot have findings: it was never linted.
must-not:\[target\] \[lint-kyverno#1/
